// REJECTS: Unexpected token 'fields'. Valid alternatives: ['properties']
//
// `fields:` inside an `Object` property. Elasticsearch splits its property bodies in two:
// scalarPropertyContent admits only `fields:` (sub-fields of a leaf, e.g. an analysed Text
// alongside a raw Keyword) and complexPropertyContent admits only `properties:` (the members
// of an Object or Nested). The two keywords are not interchangeable in either direction --
// `properties:` under a `Keyword` is refused with the mirror-image message.
//
// The property TYPE selects which body rule applies, so the constraint is a dependency between
// a type token and the field names legal after it, one rule away. A rewrite with a single
// property-body parser -- accepting both keywords and sorting it out later, which costs
// nothing and produces a valid-looking protocol object either way -- is more permissive here
// and its output is a mapping Elasticsearch itself would reject.
//
// ../fixtures/t2-elasticsearch.pure writes both shapes for exactly this reason.
###Elasticsearch
Elasticsearch7Cluster fixture::neg::FieldsOnObject
{
  indices: [
    index1: {
      properties: [
        prop1: Object {
          fields: [
            raw: Keyword
          ];
        }
      ];
    }
  ];
}
